{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1276904-types-of-hollow-ones","id":1276904,"title":"Types of Hollow Ones","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-12-26T17:46:33-08:00","id":1276904,"title":"Types of Hollow Ones","content":"Since it keeps coming up here and there, I figured it might be worth having a thread to talk about the various subcultures - modern or historic - the Hollow Ones might make their home in. <br /> <br /> Goth is clearly the default, but the Goth subculture contains a wide range of subgroups. One of my personal favorites is <b>Psychobilly,</b> a mash up of 50s rockabilly and 70s punk that later incorporated a lot of gothic elements into it. Bands like The Cramps, The Meteors and Mojo Nixon were part of this, and more have sprung up over the decades. One reason I like to use them is that it tends to subvert people's expectations when they come across Hollow Ones wearing 1950s style leather jackets, driving motorcycles and hot rods, and able to swing dance and mosh with equal ability. Also, a part of the subculture is frequent the sort of cheesy and weird sci-fi and horror films that used to populate late night UHF channels and early cable, which I feel works wonderfully with technomancy. Laser blasters, weird alien pets, and psychic powers are just a few of the things they can use. <br /> <br /> <b>Queercore</b> (or Homocore) is another outgrowth/offshoot of the 70s punk movement. A number of punk bands had members who were openly out gay men, and others were vocally anti-gaybashing/homophobic. What differentiated them from the more orthodox gay culture of the time was that they didn't self-segregate themselves and tended to hang out in social groups and settings that spanned the sexuality spectrum. There was also a rejection of mainstream consumerist culture and political opposition to political and religious oppression of gays and other groups. There was a really strong Do It Yourself mentality tied to it back in the 80s, with members creating their own media outside of regular publishing and production, making or repairing their own clothes, growing their own food, and other activities. This sort of mindset is perfect for Mages who are self-taught and are willing to incorporate any new techniques they come across into their magic style. (You see a little of this in some of the early Hellblazer stories.)<br /> <br /> <b>Creepers</b>, or urban explorers, are a really interesting subculture, exploring the abandoned corners of the modern industrial landscape. Essentially picking over the bones of modern ruins in search of the macabre, grotesque or just cool, they are tailor made for the Hollow Ones' view of the world. Delving into places like the Parisian catacombs, lost subway tunnels or abandoned hospitals takes on even more risk (and potential reward) in the World of Darkness, as one never knows when you might stumble across a forgotten chantry, vampire warren or haunting. The fact that some urban explorers also double as ghost chasers or parapsychologists only adds to the potential. I personally love this as an option for Hollowers who focus on Spirit magic, acting as mediums and psychopomps. <br /> <br /> Those are three I happen to like. Anyone else have some?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-27T11:02:35-08:00","id":1277036,"title":"","content":"I feel like Goth could use some subdivision maybe or some other group. There are both ironic (Post-modern) and earnest (Romantic) goths (or maybe goth is the wrong word and it should just be romantics, Neville Nevermore types). <br /> <br /> I also feel there's a space for those who preach a more active gospel of hollowness that the search for meaning must be undertaken again. This isn't nihilism but a statement of &quot;everything to now has failed, we must look anew&quot;. These tend to be more antiquarian types.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15603-phillycuriosity","name":"PhillyCuriosity","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15603_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-27T14:32:39-08:00","id":1277081,"title":"","content":"I would very much prefer for Hollow Ones to focus on the dark romanticism of the 1800s and early 1900s ( and later years ) , rather than on the fact that many sub-cultures and styles can be and have been incorporated into the outlook(s) and approach(es) of this Craft. The various sub-groups should be brought into this dark romanticism, forlorn seeking, and thoughtful ruminations ; so that this perspective and style becomes more developed, defined, explored, and refined.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar